<file path="docs/cloudflare-hybrid-setup.md">
# Detail hybrid backend setup

Firebase Authentication remains the source of identity. Cloudflare Workers,
D1, and R2 provide the application API, relational data, and photo storage.
The mobile environment chooses each provider, so Firebase Functions or Storage
can be restored later without changing app code.

## Run everything locally first

You do not need a Cloudflare payment method, a remote D1 database, or a remote
R2 bucket for local development. Wrangler simulates both D1 and R2 on your
computer and keeps their data in local state.

```powershell
Copy-Item cloudflare\.dev.vars.example cloudflare\.dev.vars
npx wrangler d1 migrations apply detail --local --persist-to .wrangler\detail-local --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
npx wrangler dev --local --persist-to .wrangler\detail-local --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
```

The Worker will print a local address, normally `http://localhost:8787`.
For an Android emulator use `http://10.0.2.2:8787` in the mobile `.env`; for
the iOS simulator use `http://localhost:8787`. A physical phone needs an HTTPS
tunnel to your computer, because `localhost` on the phone means the phone
itself, not your development machine.

```env
EXPO_PUBLIC_BACKEND_PROVIDER=cloudflare
EXPO_PUBLIC_STORAGE_PROVIDER=cloudflare-r2
EXPO_PUBLIC_CLOUDFLARE_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8787
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAP_PROVIDER=mapbox
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=pk.your_public_mapbox_token
EXPO_PUBLIC_SIMULATE_FUNCTIONS=false
```

Local D1/R2 state is deliberately separate from the future deployed backend.
When you are ready to publish, create real Cloudflare resources, replace the
placeholder D1 ID in the Wrangler file, apply migrations with `--remote`, and
deploy.

## Create the Cloudflare resources

From the repository root:

```powershell
npx wrangler login
npx wrangler d1 create detail
npx wrangler r2 bucket create detail-photos
```

Copy the D1 database ID printed by the create command into
`cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml`. Then apply the schema and deploy:

```powershell
npx wrangler d1 migrations apply detail --remote --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
npx wrangler secret put PESEPAY_INTEGRATION_KEY --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
npx wrangler secret put PESEPAY_ENCRYPTION_KEY --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
npx wrangler secret put PESEPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
npx wrangler deploy --config cloudflare/wrangler.detail-api.toml
```

`PESEPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET` must be sent by PesePay as
`x-detail-webhook-secret`; if PesePay supplies a signed webhook scheme for
this merchant account, replace that shared-secret check with their documented
signature validation before enabling real payments.

Set PesePay's result/webhook URL to:

```text
https://detail-api.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev/v1/payments/pesepay/webhook
```

The Worker deliberately does not simulate checkout: `createPesePayPayment`
returns a configuration error until the merchant's documented checkout request
and webhook signature scheme are supplied. That exchange belongs in the Worker
only; never put either PesePay secret in the Expo environment.

## Mobile environment

Add the deployed worker address to `mobile/.env`:

```env
EXPO_PUBLIC_BACKEND_PROVIDER=cloudflare
EXPO_PUBLIC_STORAGE_PROVIDER=cloudflare-r2
EXPO_PUBLIC_CLOUDFLARE_API_URL=https://detail-api.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAP_PROVIDER=mapbox
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=pk.your_public_mapbox_token
EXPO_PUBLIC_SIMULATE_FUNCTIONS=false
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE=false
```

Keep the existing Firebase public configuration because the app still signs in
with Firebase Auth and the Worker verifies those Firebase ID tokens.

To roll back a provider without a code change:

```env
EXPO_PUBLIC_BACKEND_PROVIDER=firebase
EXPO_PUBLIC_STORAGE_PROVIDER=firebase
EXPO_PUBLIC_MAP_PROVIDER=google
```

The Worker currently owns the callable-operation path. Screens that still use
direct Firestore listeners must be migrated to `callFunction`/Worker queries
before Cloudflare can be the sole data source; do not delete Firestore or its
rules until that screen-by-screen migration is complete.
</file>

<file path="docs/didit-kyc-setup.md">
# Didit cleaner identity verification

Cleaner registration uses a hosted Didit verification session. Didit collects identity documents and any workflow-required liveness information. Detail stores the provider session reference and decision status in the private Supabase `privateKyc` table; the mobile app never receives the Didit API key. A cleaner role is granted only after the server confirms approval, the cleaner completes their application, and accepts the Cleaner Agreement.

Zimbabwe is listed on Didit's country coverage page: <https://didit.me/solutions/countries/zimbabwe/>. Configure the Zimbabwe document checks and workflow in the Didit dashboard. Verify current pricing/quotas, accepted document types, data retention, and controller/processor terms with Didit before enabling live checks.

## Configure and deploy

1. Create a Didit API key and Zimbabwe verification workflow in the Didit dashboard.
2. Store `DIDIT_API_KEY` and `DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID` in Supabase Edge Function secrets (`supabase secrets set ...`). Never add either value to an Expo or Vite client variable.
3. Deploy the Supabase migrations and `marketplace-api` Edge Function. The mobile app starts a session and can refresh its decision; approval is checked by server-side code.
4. In the app, select **Become a Cleaner**, accept the identity notice, complete the hosted check, return to Detail, and refresh if the provider is still processing. After approval, complete the cleaner application and accept the Cleaner Agreement.

Test with Didit's sandbox workflow before using live verification. Rejected or pending decisions must never grant cleaner access. Detail does not store the raw identity document images. Review the in-app privacy notice against the selected workflow and Didit's current retention and processing terms before production use.
</file>

<file path="docs/ios-app-store-readiness.md">
# iOS App Store readiness

Detail now has an iOS EAS production build profile, the `com.detail.app` bundle identifier, Sign in with Apple capability, foreground location and camera purpose strings, an in-app privacy policy, and an in-app account deletion flow. The production profile does not enable the client-side simulator. A production EAS build requires Mapbox public and native SDK download tokens; Supabase, Paynow, Didit, email/SMS, and signing configuration must also be set in their server-side or EAS environments.

Before submission, complete these Apple-side items:

- Enroll the organization in the [Apple Developer Program](https://developer.apple.com/programs/), register the App ID and Sign in with Apple capability, and configure distribution credentials in EAS.
- Create the app in [App Store Connect](https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/), then add the product name, description, category, support URL, privacy-policy URL, age rating, screenshots for the required device sizes, and review notes/test account.
- Complete [App Privacy details](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/app-privacy-details/) accurately for Supabase Auth, identity verification, precise booking location, photos, phone/email, analytics if enabled, and payment processing.
- Review the [App Review Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/) for user-generated content, service marketplaces, payments, account deletion, privacy, and required moderation/support flows. Cleaning services are physical services, so do not add App Store in-app purchase for the booking charge; verify current policy and local payment obligations before release.
- Build and install a production-signed iOS artifact, test sign-in, account deletion, booking/cancellation, notifications, location permissions, camera/library permissions, payment status and payout support flows on real devices, then submit through EAS Submit or Transporter.

The environment blocks direct requests to `developer.apple.com` with an outbound proxy 403, so the official pages above were not live-verified during this implementation. Confirm the current review requirements and App Store Connect privacy declarations before submitting. No Apple team ID, signing credentials, support URL, screenshots, or reviewer account were present in the workspace, so those release inputs cannot be completed from source code alone.
</file>

<file path="docs/live-api-credentials.md">
# Live API credentials

The current clients and backend use Supabase for authentication, database, private media, and Edge Functions. Provider credentials are never stored in the repository. Configure public client values in the app build environment and private keys in Supabase's secret store.

| Service | Values | Where to get/configure them |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Supabase client | `EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`, `EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`, `VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` | Create a project in the [Supabase dashboard](https://supabase.com/dashboard/projects); copy the project URL and publishable/anon key from **Project Settings → API**. Client keys are public; RLS protects data. |
| Supabase deployment | `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF`, `SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD` | Create a [Supabase personal access token](https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens); find the ref and database password in project settings. Store them in the GitHub `production` environment. |
| Supabase server access | `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` | Project **Settings → API**. Configure only with `supabase secrets set` or a protected local operator shell. Never put it in a Vite/Expo variable. |
| Paynow BuySafe and wallet top-ups | `PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_ID`, `PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_KEY`, optional `PAYNOW_RETURN_URL` | Create/view a merchant integration in the [Paynow Customer Portal](https://www.paynow.co.zw/). Set the result URL to `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/functions/v1/paynow-webhook`, keep BuySafe enabled, and add credentials to Supabase Edge Function secrets. Paynow settles to Detail; cleaner payouts remain manual. |
| Didit cleaner identity checks | `DIDIT_API_KEY`, `DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID` | Create API credentials and a Zimbabwe identity workflow in the [Didit dashboard](https://business.didit.me/); check [Zimbabwe coverage](https://didit.me/solutions/countries/zimbabwe/) and the [API docs](https://docs.didit.me/). Store values in Supabase Edge Function secrets. |
| Waitlist protection | Browser `VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY`, server `TURNSTILE_SECRET` | Create a widget in [Cloudflare Turnstile](https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/turnstile). Put the site key in the site build environment and the secret in Supabase Edge Function secrets. Both are required for the production waitlist. |
| Transactional email | `RESEND_API_KEY` | Create a key in [Resend](https://resend.com/api-keys) and verify `detailapp.co.zw` under [Domains](https://resend.com/domains). Store the key in Supabase Edge Function secrets. |
| Google sign-in | Supabase Google provider credentials; `EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID`, `EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID` | Configure Google in Supabase **Authentication → Sign In / Providers** and create OAuth clients in [Google Cloud Credentials](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials). Use the app's production package/bundle IDs and registered redirect URLs. |
| Apple sign-in | Supabase Apple provider credentials and iOS capability | Configure the Apple provider in Supabase and create the service ID/key in the [Apple Developer portal](https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/identifiers/list). Set the app's Sign in with Apple capability. |
| SMS sign-in | Twilio Account SID, Auth Token, and Messaging Service SID | Create a [Twilio account](https://www.twilio.com/try-twilio), configure a Messaging Service and Zimbabwe-capable sender in the [Twilio Console](https://console.twilio.com/), then enter credentials in Supabase **Authentication → Sign In / Providers → Phone → Twilio**. Test delivery to local `+263` numbers, sender registration, and pricing before production. |
| Transactional SMS and push outbox | `TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID`, `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN`, `TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID` (or `TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER`), `CRON_SECRET` | Use the same Twilio Console account and Messaging Service as phone auth. Add the credentials as Supabase Edge Function secrets. The API dispatches queued alerts immediately when `CRON_SECRET` is configured; configure GitHub repository secrets `SUPABASE_URL` and `CRON_SECRET` so the checked-in worker also retries failures every five minutes and records Expo push receipts. |
| Cleaner payout encryption | `PAYOUT_ENCRYPTION_KEY` | Generate a dedicated 32-byte key with `openssl rand -base64 32`; store it only as a Supabase Edge Function secret and keep a protected backup in your secret manager. Payout requests fail closed until this key exists. Rotate only with a planned re-encryption of stored destinations. |
| Mobile maps | `EXPO_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `MAPBOX_DOWNLOADS_TOKEN` | Create a public token with minimum map style/font read scopes and a secret token with `DOWNLOADS:READ` in the [Mapbox account dashboard](https://account.mapbox.com/access-tokens/). Set the public token in the mobile EAS build environment and the download token as an EAS secret. Both tokens are required for EAS development, preview, and production builds. The app requires a custom development/production build; Expo Go does not include Mapbox's native SDK. |
| Site deployment | `FTP_SERVER`, `FTP_USERNAME`, `FTP_PASSWORD`, `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` | Keep existing Webzim FTPS values in the GitHub `production` environment. Add Supabase URL as an environment variable and anon/publishable key as a secret for the site build. |

Supabase supplies `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, and `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` to Edge Functions. Set provider secrets through the CLI or dashboard, for example:

```sh
supabase secrets set PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_ID=... PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_KEY=... DIDIT_API_KEY=... DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID=... TURNSTILE_SECRET=... RESEND_API_KEY=... TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=... TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=... TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=... PAYOUT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=... CRON_SECRET=...
```

For the initial dashboard administrator, first create and verify the account through Supabase Auth, then run from a protected local shell:

```sh
cd functions
SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co \
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<service-role-key> \
ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com \
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_CONFIRM=YES \
npm run bootstrap-admin
```

Start from the checked-in [root environment example](../.env.example), [mobile example](../mobile/.env.example), and [site example](../site/.env.example). Only public keys belong in client variables. Do not commit `.env` files or paste service-role/provider secrets into chat or source files. For migration access and cutover details, see [Supabase cutover](supabase-cutover.md).
</file>

<file path="docs/supabase-cutover.md">
# Firebase to Supabase cutover

The application clients and the new privileged API target Supabase. Firebase remains the source system until a deliberate data migration and release cutover; do not disable the old project before the new project is configured and verified.

## Create and configure Supabase

Create a project in [Supabase](https://supabase.com/dashboard/projects), then set the project URL and publishable/anon key in `mobile/.env` and `site/.env`. Never expose the `service_role` key in either client. Apply database changes with the Supabase CLI (`supabase link --project-ref …`, then `supabase db push`) and deploy the `marketplace-api`, `paynow-webhook`, `paynow-return`, and `marketing-unsubscribe` Edge Functions. The local project configuration lives in `supabase/config.toml`.

For GitHub Actions, configure the `SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF` and `SUPABASE_URL` environment variables plus `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD`, and `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` secrets in the `production` environment. The dashboard deploy workflow also needs the existing FTPS deployment credentials. The Supabase backend workflow stays skipped until a project ref is configured.

Set server secrets with `supabase secrets set`: `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_ID`, `PAYNOW_INTEGRATION_KEY`, `PAYNOW_RETURN_URL`, `DIDIT_API_KEY`, `DIDIT_WORKFLOW_ID`, `TURNSTILE_SECRET`, `RESEND_API_KEY`, and optionally `APP_RETURN_URL`. Use production secrets only in Supabase's secret store or CI secret settings.

Configure Supabase Auth email templates/SMTP and redirect URLs, then configure Google and Apple provider credentials in Auth. For phone login, configure Twilio under **Authentication → Sign In / Providers → Phone** with Account SID, Auth Token, and Messaging Service SID. Test delivery and sender requirements on local Zimbabwe `+263` numbers before enabling it for all users. The Detail-branded OTP text is set in `supabase/config.toml`; Twilio credentials belong only in the Supabase Auth provider settings, never in app variables or source control.

## Migrate legacy accounts, data, and media

Take a Firestore export and Storage inventory first. Give the migration operator Firebase Admin read access and Supabase service-role access. Set `FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, `FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET`, `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, `SUPABASE_URL`, and `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` in a protected local shell, then run:

```sh
cd functions
npm run migrate:supabase:dry-run
npm run migrate:supabase
```

The importer is restartable and never deletes from Firebase. It creates replacement Supabase auth accounts with random passwords and records the old-to-new UID mapping in `legacyAuthMappings`; current Firebase password hashes cannot be reused by Supabase Auth. Every migrated account therefore needs a secure password reset or a fresh OAuth/SMS sign-in before launch. Accounts without a verified email or reachable phone need a support recovery path. Existing auth sessions will end at cutover.

The import remaps user and booking references to new IDs, imports supported top-level collections, and copies Firebase Storage objects to the private `detail-media` bucket. Review importer output and compare per-table counts, wallet balances, payout holds, booking statuses, review counts, KYC status, and representative media URLs. Resolve invalid or rejected records before proceeding. Do not import raw KYC document images or reusable identity-provider sessions; cleaners must complete Didit verification again if the provider session cannot be safely resumed.

## Cutover sequence

1. Configure and deploy Supabase in a staging environment. Exercise email/password, email code, Google/Apple, SMS, cleaner verification, bookings, chat, cancellation, ratings, wallet top-up, Paynow BuySafe status polling/callbacks, and manual cleaner payouts.
2. Schedule a short write freeze. Run the final import, reconcile ledger totals and record counts, and test account recovery with a migrated user. Keep Firebase data intact and available for rollback investigation.
3. Release mobile and dashboard builds with the Supabase URL/anon key. Watch Auth, database, storage, and Edge Function logs. Re-enable writes only after smoke tests pass.
4. Paynow pays the Detail merchant account; cleaner payouts remain manual via the existing payout request and admin settlement flow. BuySafe escrow release does not create a Paynow split or automatically transfer funds to a cleaner.
5. Keep the Firebase project and its backups until the rollback window closes and privacy retention obligations are met. Then remove unused Firebase runtime resources deliberately.

The Supabase free plan is suitable for a low-volume pilot, but includes quotas and operational limits that can change. Monitor database/storage/egress/Auth usage and establish backups and a paid-plan trigger before relying on it for production financial records. No production project credentials are present in this repository, so migration, provider delivery, and live callback verification require the project owner to configure secrets and run the documented deployment.
</file>

<file path="functions/scripts/bootstrap-admin.mjs">
/**
 * One-time admin bootstrap. Uses Application Default Credentials or
 * GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS. Passwords are accepted only through the
 * process environment and are never written to this repository/Firestore.
 *
 * ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com ADMIN_PASSWORD='temporary-secret' npm run bootstrap-admin
 */
import admin from 'firebase-admin';

admin.initializeApp();
const email=(process.env.ADMIN_EMAIL||'').trim().toLowerCase();
const password=process.env.ADMIN_PASSWORD||'';
if(!/^\S+@\S+\.\S+$/.test(email)||password.length<8)throw new Error('Set ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_PASSWORD (minimum 8 characters).');
let user;
try{user=await admin.auth().getUserByEmail(email);await admin.auth().updateUser(user.uid,{password,emailVerified:true,disabled:false});}
catch(error){if(error?.code!=='auth/user-not-found')throw error;user=await admin.auth().createUser({email,password,emailVerified:true});}
await admin.auth().setCustomUserClaims(user.uid,{admin:true,elevated:true,permissions:['admins.manage','marketing.read','marketing.send','finance.read','audit.read']});
process.stdout.write(`Bootstrapped elevated admin ${email} (${user.uid}). Sign in and change the temporary password immediately.\n`);
</file>

<file path="functions/scripts/bootstrap-supabase-admin.mjs">
import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';

const url = process.env.SUPABASE_URL;
const serviceRoleKey = process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY;
const email = process.env.ADMIN_EMAIL?.trim().toLowerCase();
const confirmed = process.env.BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_CONFIRM === 'YES';
if (!url || !serviceRoleKey || !email || !confirmed) {
  throw new Error('Set SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, ADMIN_EMAIL, and BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_CONFIRM=YES in a protected shell.');
}

const client = createClient(url, serviceRoleKey, { auth: { persistSession: false, autoRefreshToken: false } });
let found = null;
for (let page = 1; !found; page++) {
  const { data, error } = await client.auth.admin.listUsers({ page, perPage: 1000 });
  if (error) throw error;
  found = data.users.find(user => user.email?.toLowerCase() === email) ?? null;
  if (found || data.users.length < 1000) break;
}
if (!found) throw new Error(`No Supabase Auth account exists for ${email}. Create and verify that account first.`);

const { error } = await client.from('adminroles').upsert({
  userid: found.id,
  elevated: true,
  permissions: ['admins.manage', 'marketing.read', 'marketing.send', 'finance.read', 'audit.read'],
}, { onConflict: 'userid' });
if (error) throw error;
console.log(`Granted the initial elevated administrator role to ${email} (${found.id}). Sign in and create restricted admin invitations from the dashboard.`);
</file>

<file path="functions/scripts/migrate-firebase-to-supabase.mjs">
/**
 * One-shot, restartable Firestore/Auth/Storage import for the Supabase cutover.
 * Requires Firebase Admin credentials and a Supabase service role key.
 * Run first with --dry-run; this script never deletes from Firebase.
 */
import { createHash, randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';
import admin from 'firebase-admin';
import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';

const required = ['FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID', 'FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET', 'SUPABASE_URL', 'SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY'];
for (const key of required) if (!process.env[key]) throw new Error(`Missing ${key}`);
const dryRun = process.argv.includes('--dry-run');
admin.initializeApp({ projectId: process.env.FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID, storageBucket: process.env.FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET });
const firestore = admin.firestore();
const bucket = admin.storage().bucket();
const supabase = createClient(process.env.SUPABASE_URL, process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, { auth: { persistSession: false, autoRefreshToken: false } });

const uuid = value => {
  const bytes = createHash('sha256').update(`detail-migration:${value}`).digest().subarray(0, 16);
  bytes[6] = (bytes[6] & 0x0f) | 0x50; bytes[8] = (bytes[8] & 0x3f) | 0x80;
