<file path="storage.rules">
rules_version = '2';
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {

    function isAuth() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    function isImage() {
      return request.resource.contentType.matches('image/.*');
    }

    function underSizeLimit() {
      return request.resource.size < 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10 MB
    }

    // Booking photos ("Add Photos" step) — uploaded by the customer before a
    // cleanerId even exists, so any signed-in user may read (cleaners need to
    // see them to bid) but only the uploader's own uid segment is writable.
    match /job-photos/{uid}/{allPaths=**} {
      allow read: if isAuth();
      allow write: if isAuth() && request.auth.uid == uid && isImage() && underSizeLimit();
    }

    // Chat attachments, scoped per job. Any authenticated participant can
    // read; write is allowed for any signed-in user since job membership is
    // enforced by the Firestore `messages` rule the client writes through.
    match /chat-photos/{jobId}/{allPaths=**} {
      allow read: if isAuth();
      allow write: if isAuth() && isImage() && underSizeLimit();
    }

    // Profile photos.
    match /profile-photos/{uid}/{allPaths=**} {
      allow read: if isAuth();
      allow write: if isAuth() && request.auth.uid == uid && isImage() && underSizeLimit();
    }

    // Proof-of-residence photos captured during cleaner registration
    // (register-cleaner.tsx). Sensitive PII — unlike profile-photos, only
    // the owner can ever read their own upload back; Detail staff review it
    // via the Admin SDK (bypasses rules), not through this path.
    match /verification/{uid}/{allPaths=**} {
      allow read: if isAuth() && request.auth.uid == uid;
      allow write: if isAuth() && request.auth.uid == uid && isImage() && underSizeLimit();
    }

    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
</file>

<file path="functions/README.md">
# Legacy Firebase backend and migration tools

The mobile app and dashboard now target Supabase. `src/index.ts` and the
Firebase deployment configuration are retained only as legacy reference during
the cutover window. Do not deploy this runtime. The Firebase Admin SDK remains
for the one-time import script below.

See [`../docs/supabase-cutover.md`](../docs/supabase-cutover.md) for Supabase
setup, provider configuration, migration sequence, and rollback guidance.

## One-time Firebase to Supabase import

With Firebase Admin read credentials and the Supabase service role configured
in a protected shell, run `npm run migrate:supabase:dry-run` and review its
counts. Then use `npm run migrate:supabase` during the approved write freeze.
The importer preserves the Firebase source and marks migrated users for account
recovery. It is not a substitute for reconciliation and a staging rehearsal.

The documentation below this point describes the retired Firebase runtime and
is retained for historical reference only.

Server-side logic that the client cannot be trusted to do (moving money between
users, computing rating averages, talking to PesePay with secret keys).

| Function | Type | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `settleJobPayment` | callable | Customer pays a finished job. WALLET = atomic debit/credit + ledger; CASH = mark paid. |
| `submitRating` | callable | Writes a review and recomputes the cleaner's `ratingAverage` / `ratingCount`. |
| `acceptBid` | callable | Customer accepts a quote; sets the authoritative price and auto-declines every other outstanding bid on the job. |
| `counterOffer` | callable | Customer or cleaner proposes a new price on an open bid; appended to the bid's `history`. |
| `createPesePayPayment` | callable | Starts a PesePay hosted checkout for a wallet top-up; writes a PENDING transaction. |
| `pesepayWebhook` | HTTP | PesePay calls this on completion; verifies, then credits the wallet (idempotent). |
| `onBidCreated` | Firestore trigger | New quote → notifies the job's customer (in-app + push). |
| `onJobUpdated` | Firestore trigger | Job status change (en route / arrived / in progress / completed / cancelled) → notifies the other party. |
| `onMessageCreated` | Firestore trigger | New chat message → notifies whichever participant didn't send it. |

Notifications are written to `/notifications/{id}` (readable only by the recipient) and,
when the recipient has an `expoPushToken` on their user doc, also sent as an Expo push
through `https://exp.host/--/api/v2/push/send`. The mobile app registers its token by
writing `expoPushToken` on its own `/users/{uid}` doc — no callable required, since it's
not one of the trust/money fields locked to Cloud Functions in `firestore.rules`.

## One-time setup

```bash
# From the repo root
npm install -g firebase-tools
firebase login

# Put your project id in .firebaserc (replace YOUR_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID)
firebase use --add

cd functions
npm install
```

## PesePay secrets (never commit these)

```bash
firebase functions:secrets:set PESEPAY_INTEGRATION_KEY
firebase functions:secrets:set PESEPAY_ENCRYPTION_KEY
```

In your PesePay dashboard, set the **Result URL** to:
`https://us-central1-<PROJECT_ID>.cloudfunctions.net/pesepayWebhook`

## Deploy

```bash
# Functions only
firebase deploy --only functions

# Or everything (rules + indexes + functions) together
firebase deploy
```

## Local testing (optional)

```bash
cd functions
npm run serve   # builds + starts the emulator
```

> The mobile app runs these in PROD builds (`__DEV__ === false`). In DEV / Expo Go
> the app simulates payment & rating locally, so you don't need the functions
> deployed just to click through the UI — but you do need them for real money flow.
</file>

